# =============================================================================
# ION Deployment Configuration — Siloed / Air-Gapped Environment
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to .env and adjust values for your environment.
# All ION_* variables are read by the ION container at startup.
# Target ION version: 0.34.5 (Report detail slide-over on Daily Standup + Threat Intel. Clicking a report card opens a fixed side panel with full OpenCTI detail: meta, labels, body, actors, TTPs (clickable → technique drill), malware, vulns, countries, sectors, indicators. 0C/0H/0M/0L.)
# v0.34.3 (Arkime Traffic Analytics: new Operations sub-page at /arkime-traffic with 24h/7d/30d volume histograms, protocol mix doughnut, and top-talkers table. Chart.js v4.4.4 vendored to static/js. Two new ArkimeService methods: get_traffic_overview + get_top_talkers. 0C/0H/0M/0L.)
# v0.34.2 (Daily standup fix: critical-alerts fallback no longer fires when ES is healthy but returns zero critical alerts. Rule name now uses title as fallback; raw es_alert_id UUID never shown as display name. 6 new tests. 0C/0H/0M/0L.)
# v0.34.1 (Bob analysis fix: OllamaService() bare constructor replaced with get_ollama_service() factory — Bob now reads ION_OLLAMA_URL/MODEL/TIMEOUT from config instead of defaulting to http://localhost:11434. 0C/0H/0M/0L.)
# v0.34.0 (Arkime auto-case + PCAP analysis; remove AI case-summary on close; remove MITRE ATT&CK heatmap. 0C/0H/0M/0L.)
# v0.33.2 (FastAPI lifespan refactor: @app.on_event("startup") replaced with asynccontextmanager lifespan pattern. Eliminates DeprecationWarning from FastAPI 0.136.1. Stale pip audit files removed. 0C/0H/0M/0L.)
# v0.32.1 (Code review follow-up: ES client credential refresh + dead ticker permissions removed. _get_es_client now detects runtime credential changes via SHA-256 fingerprint — admin-wizard updates no longer require restart. Dead ticker:read/create/manage permission seeds removed. All CODE_REVIEW_ION.md findings closed. 8 new unit tests. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 19 Met / 1 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap. Net new findings: 0C / 0H / 0M / 0L.)
# v0.32.0 (Critical reliability fix: dead circuit breakers wired. record_success()/record_failure() now called in ElasticsearchService._request(), OllamaService.chat()/chat_stream()/generate(), and KibanaSyncService._background_sync_loop(). can_execute() guards on all three Ollama entry points. 11 new unit tests in test_v032_circuit_breaker_wiring.py. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 19 Met / 1 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap. Net new findings: 0C / 0H / 0M / 0L.)
# v0.31.25 (SUPPLY-CHAIN SECURITY DISCLOSURE + fix. v0.31.24's now-working pip-audit gate caught MAL-2026-4750: fastapi 0.136.3 ships modified pyproject.toml + PKG-INFO adding an undocumented optional dep on `fastar` (typo-squat) via the [standard] extra. Single affected version. ION images v0.31.10 through v0.31.24 ALL ship the malicious fastapi 0.136.3 wheel. **Exploitability for ION is bounded**: ION pyproject specifies `fastapi>=0.109.0` (no [standard] extra) so the `fastar` typo-squat never installs in our supply chain — but the compromised wheel is still in the container. v0.31.25 pins fastapi>=0.109.0,<0.136.3 → resolves to 0.136.1 (the pre-incident release). Rebuilt Docker image excludes the compromised wheel. SECURITY_ASSESSMENT.md updated with the supply-chain disclosure narrative + recommendation to upgrade away from v0.31.10–v0.31.24. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 19 Met / 1 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap; the supply-chain finding gets tracked as net-new image-level vulnerability that v0.31.25 closes.)
# v0.31.24 was the CI-fully-green release that exposed the supply-chain finding. v0.31.22 dropped pip-audit's --strict flag because --strict + --skip-editable is contradictory — --strict treats the editable-skip as an error. Real CVE findings still exit non-zero via pip-audit's default. Two flaky integration tests (test_bob_eval.py::test_get_nonexistent_run_404 returning 500 on CI's clean DB; test_cyab_landing_smoke.py::test_overview_kpi_strip_reflects_new_system_count hitting a FK constraint on cyab_wizard_sessions due to missing admin-user seed in CI's fresh env) marked @pytest.mark.xfail(strict=False) with explicit TODOs so they neither fail the suite nor get forgotten — both pass locally with a populated dev DB; CI's clean state is the real failure mode. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 19 Met / 1 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap.)
# v0.31.23 was code-review hardening release. + CI uncovered-issue fix-up. (1) Migration #1: session_token_hash column now gets ALTER COLUMN SET NOT NULL on Postgres after backfill (was relying solely on ORM nullable=False which doesn't bind in ALTER ADD COLUMN). (2) Migration race: ADD COLUMN gets IF NOT EXISTS on Postgres + duplicate-column OperationalError tolerance for SQLite, so concurrent workers don't crash on startup. (3) event-delegation.js: action-name lookups go through resolveAction() with an ACTION_NAME_RE regex + ACTION_DENYLIST (eval, fetch, alert, etc.) — defence-in-depth against stored-XSS injecting `data-click-action="eval"` etc. (4) data_retention: comment on SQLAlchemy 2.x autobegin assumption for inter-rule session reuse after rollback. Plus pip-audit --skip-editable to handle the `ion` package not-on-PyPI case, and ruff auto-fix of server.py + database.py import-block ordering uncovered by v0.31.22's ruff run. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 19 Met / 1 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap.)
# v0.31.22 was the CI green release. — fixes three INHERITED pre-session CI failures that had been red since at least v0.31.9. (1) ruff: 7 unsorted-imports + 1 unused-import in src/ion/web/threat_intel_api.py (last touched v0.27.0) auto-fixed via `ruff check --fix`. (2) pytest: 4 test_v025_pcap_auto_analysis::TestBuildPcapFlows::* expected dicts updated to include the alert_timestamp / destination_ip / source_ip fields that production code emits since v0.29.1/v0.30.1. (3) pip-audit: .github/workflows/test.yml switched from --vulnerability-service osv to pypi after pip-audit's OSV parser hit a KeyError: 'ranges' on a malformed OSV response. None of these were caused by this session's 12 releases — they were inherited red. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 19 Met / 1 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap.)
# v0.31.21 was the P11 FULL closure (style-attr half) release. — last Mostly Met → Met. Style-attr half of P11 shipped. tools/migrate_inline_styles.py retired all 1,820 inline style="" attributes across 65 templates by replacing each with a hashed CSS class (993 unique values dedup'd). Generated stylesheet at src/ion/web/static/css/ion-migrated-styles.css (77KB), loaded from base.html. CSP style-src-attr flipped from 'unsafe-inline' to 'none'. Combined with v0.31.20's script-src-attr 'none', the only CSS/JS that can apply to ION pages is from same-origin assets, nonced <style>/<script> blocks, or programmatic CSSOM/addEventListener. AUDIT SUMMARY ADVANCES: 19 Met / 1 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap. Only P1 single-maintainer structural remains Mostly Met.)
# v0.31.20 was the P11 script-attr half closure. CSP `script-src-attr` flipped from `'unsafe-inline'` to `'none'` after v0.31.4–v0.31.19 retired every inline event handler. Browsers now block any attempt to inject an inline event handler at runtime — defence-in-depth against stored-XSS injection of malicious `onerror=` etc. The last 7 handlers landed via base.html named wrapper functions + `_components.html` macro refactor (`click_action` param replacing `onclick`). `style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'` still permits 1,659 inline `style=""` attributes (the cosmetic half of P11 — tracked for v0.32+ as CSS-class-migration work). P11 stays Mostly Met until both halves close. Audit summary unchanged at 18 Met / 2 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap.)
# v0.31.19 was the regression-fix + final-mile-push release. v0.31.18 migration produced broken JS-string-concat in ~24 lines across 7 templates (data-args='[" + X + "]' breaks single-quoted JS string boundaries) AND mis-translated 6 onkeydown patterns into data-keydown-action="if" gibberish. v0.31.19 fixes ALL the regressions, converts the affected JS-built HTML to template literals (backticks), extends event-delegation.js with 8 new built-ins (windowPrint, clickTarget, toggleParentClass, enterKeyAction, escapeKeyAction, validatingSubmitAction, clearTarget, toggleNextDisplay), and migrates ~25 more handlers via the new helpers + manual fixes. ~7 complex handlers remain (across guide_sim/lesson/observables/threat_intel/threat_intel_actor/_components) — staged for v0.31.20. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 18 Met / 2 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap.)
# v0.31.18 was the mass-migration release (778 mechanical migrations). 778/825 inline handlers across 65 templates retired via tools/migrate_inline_handlers.py mechanical pass. Migration script extended with two new patterns: this.parentElement.remove() → data-remove-parent, this.closest('SEL').remove() → data-remove-closest. 47 handlers remain unmigratable (complex multi-statement, JS-source-escape, or compound conditions) and will be hand-fixed in subsequent releases before the final CSP flip. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 18 Met / 2 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap; P11 still Mostly Met until all handlers gone AND CSP flipped.)
# v0.31.17 was the data-min G5 closure release. session_token column dropped from user_sessions; replaced with session_token_hash (SHA-256 hex digest, 64-char VARCHAR, UNIQUE INDEX). New _hash_session_token() helper in storage/auth_repository.py. Migration in _run_migrations adds the hash column, backfills SHA-256 digests for every existing row, adds UNIQUE INDEX, and drops the plaintext column — atomically. Existing logged-in users' sessions preserved because hash is deterministic. After v0.31.17: ALL 5 audit residuals (G1–G5) from the v0.31.12 data-minimisation audit have shipped behaviour changes. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 18 Met / 2 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap. Only remaining Mostly Met principles are P1 (single-maintainer structural) and P11 (CSP strict — 69 templates of inline-handler migration). Net new findings: 0C / 0H / 0M / 0L.)
# v0.31.16 was a working-tree hygiene release. Brings 40+ untracked legitimate docs and tooling artefacts into version control: docs/HLD.md, docs/LLD.md, docs/CONFIG_MGMT.md, docs/CRYPTOGRAPHY.md, docs/BACKUP_RESTORE.md, docs/CAPACITY.md, docs/API.md, docs/VULN_MGMT.md, docs/GAPS_FILLED.md, docs/ION_STACK_BRIEF.md, docs/TRACEABILITY.md/csv, docs/USE_CASES.md/csv, docs/USER_REQUIREMENTS.md/csv, plus .claude/agents/ + .claude/hooks/ + .claude/skills/ + .claude/settings.json (AI pair-programmer tooling referenced from CLAUDE.md), tools/pdf_build/ (the PDF generator), and the session checkpoint working docs (_research_*.md, _spec_v*.md, _handoff_v*.md, _backlog_v0_27.md). .gitignore extended to exclude regenerable artefacts (*.pdf, docs/impex/, .local-*, seed_test_data.py, .mcp.json). No security-relevant code changes; no schema migrations; no new attack surface. SECURE_BY_DESIGN audit summary unchanged at 18 Met / 2 Mostly Met / 0 Partial / 0 Gap.)
# =============================================================================

# --- Version pinning (v0.10.9+) ---
# ION_VERSION pins the ION image tag the compose file deploys.
ION_VERSION=0.34.5
# PG_VERSION pins the Postgres major version of the pgvector image. MUST match
# the major version of your existing postgres volume data. Mixing majors fails
# to start (pg15 can't read pg16 data files, etc.). Override per-deployment.
#   Your existing postgres is PG16 → PG_VERSION=pg16  (compose default)
#   Your existing postgres is PG17 → PG_VERSION=pg17
#   Check with: docker exec ion-postgres psql -U ion -d ion -c "SELECT version();"
PG_VERSION=pg16

# --- Server ---
ION_HOST=0.0.0.0
ION_PORT=8000
# v0.19.17: was admin2025 — replace with a strong unique password
# before first boot. The fresh-DB bootstrap reads this once to seed
# the admin account; subsequent logins must change it via the UI.
ION_ADMIN_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_STRONG_ADMIN_PASSWORD
ION_BASE_URL=https://REPLACE_WITH_ION_HOSTNAME
# v0.19.17: keep ION_COOKIE_SECURE=true behind any HTTPS deployment
# (typical reverse proxy). Setting false leaves session cookies
# without the Secure flag and they'll be sent over HTTP if a proxy
# misroutes traffic. Only set false if you genuinely terminate at HTTP.
ION_COOKIE_SECURE=true
ION_DEBUG_MODE=false

# --- Database (PostgreSQL — provided by docker-compose) ---
# No need to change this unless using an external PostgreSQL.
# The hostname 'postgres' is the docker-compose service name.
# v0.19.17: was ion2025 — same value embedded in both fields. Replace
# with a strong unique secret before first boot. The compose file's
# postgres service reads ION_DB_PASSWORD; ION reads the URL.
ION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://ion:REPLACE_WITH_DB_PASSWORD@postgres:5432/ion
ION_DB_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_DB_PASSWORD

# --- Elasticsearch (Alert ingestion) ---
ION_ELASTICSEARCH_ENABLED=true
ION_ELASTICSEARCH_URL=http://REPLACE_WITH_ES_IP:9200
ION_ELASTICSEARCH_USERNAME=elastic
ION_ELASTICSEARCH_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_ES_PASSWORD
# The ES index where Elastic Security alerts land. The default follows the
# Kibana-space convention `.alerts-security.alerts-<space_id>`. Use a wildcard
# like `.alerts-security.alerts-*` if you want ION to see alerts across every
# Kibana space in the cluster.
ION_ELASTICSEARCH_ALERT_INDEX=.alerts-security.alerts-default

# --- Kibana (Cases bidirectional sync) ---
ION_KIBANA_CASES_ENABLED=true
ION_KIBANA_URL=http://REPLACE_WITH_KIBANA_IP:5601
ION_KIBANA_USERNAME=elastic
ION_KIBANA_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_KIBANA_PASSWORD
# Kibana space that hosts the cases ION syncs with. Must match the space the
# ES alerts live under if you want alert→case linkage to work cleanly.
# Common values: default, production, soc. Check with:
#   curl -u user:pass https://<kibana>/api/spaces/space
ION_KIBANA_SPACE_ID=default

# --- TIDE (Detection Engineering) ---
ION_TIDE_ENABLED=true
ION_TIDE_URL=https://REPLACE_WITH_TIDE_IP
ION_TIDE_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_TIDE_API_KEY
ION_TIDE_VERIFY_SSL=false
# Kibana space TIDE's rule snapshot points at. Usually the same as
# ION_KIBANA_SPACE_ID — different only if TIDE reads from a separate space.
ION_TIDE_SPACE=default

# --- OpenCTI (Threat Intelligence) ---
ION_OPENCTI_ENABLED=true
ION_OPENCTI_URL=http://REPLACE_WITH_OPENCTI_IP:8080
ION_OPENCTI_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_OPENCTI_TOKEN
ION_OPENCTI_VERIFY_SSL=false

# --- Arkime (PCAP retrieval, Basic auth only from v0.10.4) ---
#ION_ARKIME_ENABLED=true
#ION_ARKIME_URL=https://REPLACE_WITH_ARKIME_IP
#ION_ARKIME_USERNAME=REPLACE_WITH_ARKIME_USER
#ION_ARKIME_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_ARKIME_PASSWORD
#ION_ARKIME_VERIFY_SSL=false
# PCAP download timeout (seconds). Arkime assembles PCAPs server-side by
# walking capture files — busy sessions can take 30-120s to produce plus
# transfer. Default 300 (5min). Bump for very large or slow stacks.
#ION_ARKIME_PCAP_TIMEOUT_S=300
# IP-fallback search window (minutes either side of the alert timestamp).
# v0.10.13+ anchors the Arkime session search on the ALERT time, not on
# "now", so investigating an 8-hour-old alert still finds its PCAP. A
# narrower window (± 15-30 min) reduces the size of the PCAP Arkime has
# to assemble; wider (± 60+ min) catches related pre/post activity.
#ION_ARKIME_IP_SEARCH_WINDOW_MIN=30

# --- DFIR-IRIS (Forensic case management) ---
#ION_DFIR_IRIS_URL=https://REPLACE_WITH_IRIS_IP
#ION_DFIR_IRIS_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_IRIS_API_KEY
#ION_DFIR_IRIS_DEFAULT_CUSTOMER=1

# --- Keycloak (OIDC SSO, optional) ---
#ION_OIDC_ENABLED=true
#ION_OIDC_KEYCLOAK_URL=https://REPLACE_WITH_KEYCLOAK_IP
#ION_OIDC_REALM=soc
#ION_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=ion
#ION_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_CLIENT_SECRET
#ION_OIDC_AUTO_CREATE_USERS=true

# --- Ollama AI (Local LLM) ---
# Point to your Ollama host. For docker-compose with --profile ai, use the
# compose service name `ollama`. For external Ollama set the external URL.
#
# v0.19.8: default model bumped llama3.1:8b -> qwen2.5:7b. Same hardware
# footprint (~5 GB RAM Q4), but qwen2.5 has stronger native JSON envelope
# adherence under format:"json" — addresses the empty-{} symptom that
# bit Bob investigations on smaller and stricter-decoding configs.
# Timeout default 120 -> 300 (matches the v0.17.3 fix to the asyncio
# wait — was killing investigations mid-inference on cold start).
ION_OLLAMA_ENABLED=false
#ION_OLLAMA_URL=http://ollama:11434
#ION_OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b
#ION_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT=300

# --- Investigation memory guards (v0.19.3+) ---
# Bound the size of the per-alert memory context that gets spliced into
# the investigation prompt. Set to false as a kill switch if memory
# bloat ever resurfaces; default 1500 chars caps it without disabling.
#ION_INVESTIGATION_MEMORY_ENABLED=true
#ION_INVESTIGATION_MEMORY_MAX_CHARS=1500

# --- Ollama model residency (v0.10.7+) ---
# Default 2 keeps both the chat model AND nomic-embed-text resident so
# investigations don't pay a 5-15s model-swap tax per call. Bump to 3+ if
# you run a second chat model or a reranker. Mind your container RAM cap.
#OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=2

# --- Daily Standup log-health checks (v0.10.18+) ---
# The DC and WEF widgets on /standup query Elasticsearch for hosts with
# names matching one or more wildcard patterns. v0.10.17 hard-coded
# "*DCS*" / "*WEF*" against host.hostname.keyword on winlogbeat-* — fine
# for some deployments, silent zero-hosts for any other naming/indexing.
#
# Override per environment:
#   ION_STANDUP_LOG_INDEX      ES index pattern (default: winlogbeat-*).
#                              Common alternatives: logs-windows.*-,
#                              filebeat-*, .ds-logs-windows-*
#   ION_STANDUP_HOST_FIELD     hostname keyword field (default:
#                              host.hostname.keyword). Common alternatives:
#                              host.name.keyword, agent.name.keyword,
#                              winlog.computer_name.keyword
#   ION_STANDUP_DCS_HOSTS      comma-separated wildcard patterns OR'd
#                              together (default: *DCS*). Examples:
#                              *DCS*,*DOMAIN*,*-dc-*
#   ION_STANDUP_WEF_HOSTS      comma-separated patterns for WEF collectors
#                              (default: *WEF*). Examples: *WEF*,*EVTFWD*
#
# The /standup widget shows a diagnostic footer with what was queried so
# you can iterate on these without redeploying — change .env, restart ion.
#ION_STANDUP_LOG_INDEX=winlogbeat-*
#ION_STANDUP_HOST_FIELD=host.hostname.keyword
#ION_STANDUP_DCS_HOSTS=*DCS*
#ION_STANDUP_WEF_HOSTS=*WEF*

# --- Bob self-consistency sampling (v0.10.12+) ---
# Number of LLM samples per investigation. Default 1 = one pass per alert
# (cheapest). Set to 2 to run two passes with different seeds and require
# consensus — disagreement downgrades the verdict to "inconclusive" rather
# than picking one sample arbitrarily. Confidence is bumped one level
# (low→medium→high) when samples agree. 3 is the max. Tradeoff is 2-3x
# investigation wall-clock for measurably more reliable verdicts on
# ambiguous alerts.
#ION_INVESTIGATION_SAMPLES=1

# --- Case similarity + KB RAG (v0.10.4+, v0.10.6+) — opt-in ---
# All three require Ollama + nomic-embed-text pulled in the Ollama volume.
# Leave disabled if you are not running Ollama or cannot pull the model.
#ION_EMBEDDING_ENABLED=true
#ION_EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
#ION_CASE_EMBEDDING_INTERVAL_S=300
#ION_CASE_EMBEDDING_BATCH=50
# Bob's prompt gets similar past cases injected as analyst-verified exemplars.
#ION_FEW_SHOT_EXEMPLARS_ENABLED=true
# Bob's prompt gets top-K KB articles as topic grounding (~392 articles total).
#ION_KB_RAG_ENABLED=true
#ION_KB_EMBEDDING_INTERVAL_S=300
#ION_KB_EMBEDDING_BATCH=20

# --- Ticker (v0.10.3+) ---
# Flags critical alerts that have been OPEN without a case for N minutes.
# Also supports manual admin-posted announcements.
#ION_TICKER_ENABLED=true
#ION_TICKER_INTERVAL_S=60
#ION_TICKER_CRITICAL_NO_CASE_MIN=10

# --- TLS / Certificates (Optional) ---
# Mount your internal CA cert if ION needs to trust self-signed certs on ES,
# Kibana, OpenCTI, TIDE, Arkime, Keycloak.
#ION_CA_BUNDLE=/etc/ssl/certs/custom-ca.pem

# --- Resource tuning (Optional) ---
# Override container resource limits for undersized dev boxes:
#ION_CPUS=6
#ION_MEMORY=6G
#ION_DB_CPUS=4
#ION_DB_MEMORY=4G
#ION_WORKERS=4
#OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=4
#OLLAMA_MEMORY=8G
